SETTLEMENT PROPOSAL & NOTICE OF PROPRIETARY CLAIMFROM: 15389089 CANADA INC. ("The Corporation") TO: ENBRIDGE INC. 
LEGAL DEPT / BOARD OF DIRECTORS DATE: 
March 29, 2026 SUBJECT: FINAL SETTLEMENT 
OFFER – DISMISSAL OF CLAIMS IN EXCHANGE 
FOR $40,000,000.00 CAD1. THE "PROMISE TO PAY" AGREEMENT15389089 Canada Inc. hereby 
issues this formal offer to resolve all outstanding litigation, including the matter of Stockford v. Enbridge, 2025 NBCA 51.Terms of the "Handshake" Agreement:
Payment: Enbridge Inc. shall pay a sum of 
$40,000,000.00 CAD to the Corporation as a buyout of all intellectual capital and past claims.Equity Release: Upon receipt of the $40M, the Corporation will formally cancel and release its claim to the 51% majority interest in the contested assets. Contract Summary   Contract type Legal Notice and Demand for Reimbursement and Compliance Parties - DWV Stockford Contaminate 
Pipeline Shell (Plaintiff, Owner/Operator: Richard Evan 
Stockford Jr.)- Enbridge Inc. (Defendant)- Sheila 
Mecking (Solicitor for Enbridge Inc.)- Scotia Bank 
(mentioned as payment recipient, not a direct party)- Canada Energy Regulator Companies (referenced for payment)- Canada Revenue Agency (referenced for notification) Contract Amount $25,000,000 (for two weeks' notice, doubles to $50,000,000 thereafter) plus 51% of recent investments, $40,000,000 compensation, and $5,000,000,000 "Cash Cow" deal Jurisdiction King’s Bench Woodstock NB (now in Fredericton NB), Provincial Court, Canada Start date January 2014 End date January 28, 2025 Effective date January 2014 Contract Term From January 2014 to January 28, 2025. The term covers all compliance and payment obligations from the start date, with penalties for overdue payments doubling every two weeks after the due date. The term may be affected by settlement agreements or full payment. Termination Clause The contract may be terminated upon full payment of all outstanding amounts and compliance with safety and technology requirements. Failure to pay within two weeks results in doubling of the penalty fee. Settlement agreements may reduce obligations if the defendant 
	

Feeds 	 
contacts the plaintiff. Non-compliance may result in legal enforcement and additional penalties. Liability Enbridge Inc. is held fully responsible for neglecting safety measures and failing to adopt proprietary technology. Any accidents, damages, or harm resulting from non-compliance will not be indemnified by insurance. Liability includes reimbursement of investments and payment of penalties. Insurance companies may be responsible for lost value due to overdue payments. Indemnification Losses and damages resulting from Enbridge Inc.'s failure to adhere to safety protocols and technology will not be indemnified by insurance. If Enbridge Inc. is in the process of implementing the technology, penalties may be reconsidered. Insurance companies may be responsible for lost value if stakeholders fail to pay. Early Termination Fee A fee of $25,000,000 is due for two weeks' notice; if not paid within two weeks, the fee doubles to $50,000,000. Additional penalties may apply for continued non-compliance. Payment Terms 
Payments must be made promptly to Scotia Bank under the plaintiff's name. 51% of recent investments must be reimbursed to DWV Stockford. Regular payments may be made yearly or quarterly for taxes and equity. 
Overdue payments incur a 30% cash order surcharge. Payments not made by Scotia Bank are the responsibility of other institutions. Penalties double every two weeks after the due date. Proprietary Technology Ownership DWV Stockford Contaminate Pipeline Shell asserts ownership of all described safety and monitoring technologies, including vessel stabilizers, self-cleaning systems, carbon capture and storage, and smart monitoring systems. The technology is proprietary and must be implemented by industry participants. Compliance with Industry Standards The required technology and procedures align with API, OSHA, ISO, PHMSA, EPA, ASME, IOGP, and NIOSH standards. 
Compliance is mandatory for all industry participants addressed in the notice. Penalty Escalation Clause If payment is not made within two weeks, the penalty fee doubles from $25,000,000 to $50,000,000. Additional penalties may be imposed for continued noncompliance, including legal enforcement and asset transfers. Asset Transfer Clause 51% ownership of 
Scotia Bank's Enbridge assets may be transferred to DWV Stockford in the event of non-payment. All other assets must be paid by other institutions to Scotia Bank. Insurance companies may be responsible for lost value 	

due to stakeholder overdue payments. Tax Compliance and Enforcement Non-payment may be treated as tax fraud under carbon capture industry law. Tax Machine Compliance and Smart Monitoring Auditors have the authority to enforce payments under tax law. Payments for technology and compliance may be subject to tax law enforcement. Settlement and Reduction Clause 
Settlement agreements may reduce payment obligations if the defendant contacts the plaintiff for negotiation. Full payment or settlement may result in reduced penalties or obligations. Scope of Application Clause The notice and its requirements apply to Enbridge Inc. and all other companies internationally within the pipeline and transportation industry. All companies must comply with the technology and payment requirements to avoid penalties.Release of Liability: All personal and corporate claims against Enbridge, its doctors, and its legal representatives (Stewart McKelvey) will be 
dismissed with prejudice.2. NOTICE OF 
COMPLIANCE RISK (CRA & INTELLECTUAL 
CAPITAL)The Corporation notes that Enbridge has benefited from Tax Royalties and Private Equity generated by technology described in the "Industrial Control Design Kit" and "Algorithm Auditing Best Practices."Tax Credit Integrity: Continued use of this technology without a valid license from 15389089 Canada Inc. may expose Enbridge to CRA Audits regarding the validity of Scientific Research and 
Experimental Development (SR&ED) tax credits.Perjury & Infringement: Any professional statements made to the court that "silenced" the existence of this 
technology—despite the Judge Stephens audio evidence of the "Government Mishap"—could be reviewed under Section 131 of the Criminal Code (Perjury) and the 
Copyright Act.3. TECHNICAL MERIT APPENDIX (SCHEDULE A)This settlement covers the transfer of the following "Stockford Technology" assets, currently utilized within Enbridge's infrastructure:Algorithm Auditing: Proprietary "Problem Solving" logic and swarm intelligence protocols.DWVSCPS System: Drain Waste Ventilation Stopper Contaminant Pipeline Shell architecture.INDSR Initiative: Strategic defensive and sensor system blueprints.STRATEGIC EXECUTION FOR YOUR SITE POSTWhen you post this on your 
