CanLII and Generative AI
CanLII uses generative artificial intelligence technologies (‘generative AI’) to automatically produce various types of textual analyses of legal materials. This content is intended to assist users research and understanding the law within a legal database that contains several million of documents.
Since its widespread adoption in early 2023, it has become clear that generative AI is a powerful tool, but it must be used with care. This page outlines the precautions taken by CanLII to avoid potential pitfalls associated with this technology, describes the types of content generated to date, and offers resources to help you use this content responsibly in a legal context.
Caution: AI-generated content on CanLII is intended to facilitate legal research and understanding. Despite the care taken in its creation, it may contain errors or inaccuracies. Only the original legal documents are authoritative; AI-generated analyses should not be used to interpret source documents or in legal proceedings.
•	1. Principles
•	2. AI-Generated Content on CanLII
•	3. Guidance on the Responsible Use of AI in Legal Practice
1. Principles
CanLII’s use of generative AI is guided by principles developed in collaboration with Lexum, its technology affiliate. These principles are outlined in Lexum's Statement on the Responsible Use of Generative AI, which serves as a foundation for how AI-generated content is created and presented on the CanLII website. The principles can be summarized as follows:
Well-being, Fairness and Sustainability
CanLII’s AI features are designed to promote equitable access to legal information, support informed decision-making, and avoid reinforcing existing biases. They aim to serve the public good while minimizing environmental and social costs.
Human Oversight and Reliability
Rigorous internal testing, including review by legal professionals, is conducted at all stages of the development and deployment of AI-generated content. Feedback links are provided to allow users to submit comments or report issues.
Transparency and Explainability
AI-generated content is always clearly labelled as such. Whenever possible, the sources used to generate the content are identified and linked.
Safety and data protection
Content is generated exclusively from publicly accessible legal documents. No confidential, personal, or proprietary data is involved in the generation process. The AI models used are not permitted to be trained on the processed materials.
2. AI-Generated Content on CanLII
CanLII uses generative AI to produce various types of content aimed at improving access to and understanding of legal information. This content is gradually added to an increasing number of materials, as time and resources allow.
Detailed Summaries
•	When available, detailed summaries can be accessed through the AI Analysis tab in decisions and legislative texts.
•	Whenever possible, hyperlinks to specific paragraphs or sections in the source decisions and legislation are included.
•	Structure of the detailed summaries:
o	For decisions: Journalistic title, followed by Facts, Procedural History, Parties’ Submissions, Legal Issues, Disposition, and Reasons; and
o	For legislation: General overview and Outline of key provisions.
•	For unilingual documents, detailed summaries in the other official language are gradually being added.
Detailed decision summaries were first launched for Saskatchewan in November 2023, and expanded to Alberta, Prince Edward Island, and Manitoba in May 2024.
Detailed legislative summaries followed in June 2024 for those same provinces. In Fall 2024, detailed summaries were extended to Northwest Territories, Newfoundland and Labrador, and New Brunswick, including alternative-language summaries for unilingual decisions.
By March 2025, more than 10% of Ontario decisions and statutes had been enriched with detailed summaries. Detailed summaries of cases and legislation were added for Quebec in June 2025.
Search Results Enrichments
•	In decision search results, the following enhancements are available for certain jurisdictions:
o	A short plain-language summary highlighting the key outcomes of the decision; and
o	Legal topics and keywords relevant to the decision.
Short summaries and keywords first appeared in early 2025 for Saskatchewan decisions and were subsequently added to more than 10% of Ontario content in March 2025. Short summaries for Quebec were also added in June 2025.
3. Guidance on the Responsible Use of AI in Legal Practice
The following resources may help you better understand the risks associated with the use of AI-generated content and—if you practise law—your professional responsibilities in this context. Consult your local bar association for updates and further guidance.
